    "google_books_categories": "History",
    "google_books_description": "The village of Silwan in eastern Jerusalem contains about 50 rock-cut monumental tombs dating to the period of the Judean Kingdom. This necropolis ranks among the most important and best preserved remains of the period in Jerusalem. Here were buried high officials of the Judean kingdom, among them apparently Shebna the Royal Stewart, whose rock-cut tomb is mentioned by the prophet Isaiah (22.16). The necropolis is of considerable importance for the study of ancient Jerusalem, the archaeology of the Land of Israel, Hebrew epigraphy, and the monumental funerary architecture of the ancient Near East. Though it has been a focus of scholarly interest since the nineteenth century, only the systematic survey conducted by Ussishkin between 1968 and 1971 provided an overall view of the necropolis a",
